			<item>
				<title>Syntax</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/syntax/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/syntax/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;syntax&#34;&gt;Syntax&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#syntax&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Welcome back — and no skipping! The warm-up in &lt;code&gt;exercises/01_intro/00_welcome&lt;/code&gt;&#xA;should be green before you read on.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It hardly felt like an exercise, did it? Yet those few lines smuggled in&#xA;most of Go&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;syntax&lt;/strong&gt;: packages, functions, parameters, return types.&#xA;Rather than dissecting every detail now, we&amp;rsquo;ll unpack &lt;em&gt;just enough&lt;/em&gt; to keep&#xA;you moving. The rest can wait until you actually need it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;comments&#34;&gt;Comments&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#comments&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You can use &lt;code&gt;//&lt;/code&gt; for single-line comments:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>The test workflow</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/test_workflow/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/test_workflow/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;the-test-workflow&#34;&gt;The test workflow&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#the-test-workflow&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The previous two exercises handed you tests to run. This one makes you&#xA;&lt;em&gt;read&lt;/em&gt; one. Open &lt;code&gt;add_test.go&lt;/code&gt; before touching &lt;code&gt;add.go&lt;/code&gt; — for the rest of&#xA;this course, the test file is the specification and the stub is just the&#xA;blank to fill in.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;anatomy-of-a-table-test&#34;&gt;Anatomy of a table test&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#anatomy-of-a-table-test&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;TestAdd&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;t&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;testing&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;) {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tests&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; []&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;want&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    }{&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        {&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;},&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        {&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;},&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        {&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;},&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;range&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tests&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;got&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Add&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tt&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tt&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;); &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;got&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tt&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;want&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;            &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Errorf&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Add(%d, %d) = %d, want %d&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tt&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tt&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;got&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;tt&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;want&lt;/span&gt;)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Three ideas packed into eighteen lines. Let&amp;rsquo;s take them apart.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Packages and imports</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/packages/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/packages/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;packages-and-imports&#34;&gt;Packages and imports&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#packages-and-imports&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So far every exercise lived in a single file. Real programs don&amp;rsquo;t — and Go&#xA;has opinions about how files find each other. This exercise&amp;rsquo;s stub is one&#xA;line, but it opens two doors: the standard library and the visibility rule&#xA;that governs all of Go.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;borrowing-from-the-standard-library&#34;&gt;Borrowing from the standard library&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#borrowing-from-the-standard-library&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;greet.go&lt;/code&gt; starts with a line you haven&amp;rsquo;t written yourself yet:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;strings&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;That string is an &lt;strong&gt;import path&lt;/strong&gt;, and it buys you the entire &lt;code&gt;strings&lt;/code&gt;&#xA;package: case conversion, trimming, splitting, joining, searching. Go ships&#xA;with a standard library most languages would call a framework — and using&#xA;it is always one import away, no package manager involved.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>gofmt and go vet</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/tooling/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/tooling/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;gofmt-and-go-vet&#34;&gt;gofmt and go vet&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#gofmt-and-go-vet&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Go ends a debate most languages never settle: &lt;em&gt;how should code look?&lt;/em&gt; The&#xA;answer is a tool, not an opinion. This exercise&amp;rsquo;s stub is Exhibit A — open&#xA;&lt;code&gt;tooling.go&lt;/code&gt; and look at the indentation before you fix anything.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Quote&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;That flush-left &lt;code&gt;return&lt;/code&gt; compiles perfectly. It&amp;rsquo;s also wrong — not&#xA;logically, but socially. Every Go programmer&amp;rsquo;s eyes expect a tab there, and&#xA;the toolchain enforces the expectation.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Welcome</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/welcome/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/01_intro/welcome/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;welcome&#34;&gt;Welcome&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#welcome&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You&amp;rsquo;ve heard Go is simple. Small standard library habits, fast builds, a&#xA;toolchain that does what you mean. This course tests that reputation the&#xA;only honest way: by making you write it, one exercise at a time.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You&amp;rsquo;ll go from an empty &lt;code&gt;return &amp;quot;&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt; to a working HTTP ticket API across&#xA;roughly a hundred exercises. No prior Go assumed — but you should already&#xA;know at least one programming language. Everything else, including the&#xA;unfamiliar bits, is taught when you need it.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
	</channel>
